      "id": 2,
      "text": "Before settlers arrived, the Duwamish built two longhouses along Yarrow Bay, near what is now Yarrow Point.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yarrow_Point%2C_Washington"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town officially incorporated on June 15, 1959, after decades of growth along Lake Washington.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yarrow_Point%2C_Washington"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Yarrow Point occupies a peninsula between Cozy Cove and Yarrow Bay, almost entirely north of State Route 520.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yarrow_Point%2C_Washington"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The town is bordered by Hunts Point to the west, Clyde Hill to the south, and Kirkland to the east.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yarrow_Point%2C_Washington"
    },
